WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.680
This episode of Real Engineering is brought to you by Brilliant.org:

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:04.860
a problem solving website
that helps you think like an engineer.

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:10.140
There is over four billion 
mobile phone users in the world,

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:13.580
a figure that is expected to grow to five billion 
by the end of the decade.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:16.560
We've all become hugely dependent 
on this amazing tool

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:19.500
and the feeling of it running out of battery 
is all too familiar:

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:22.735
that desperate scramble 
before it goes gently into the night.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:26.100
These devices last, at best, 
about one full day on a charge

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:29.560
and must spend the better part 
of the evening tethered to a wall.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.280
Unless you are constantly 
carrying your charger with you,

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:35.860
it is difficult to keep your phone from dying 
over the course of a busy day.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.260
But what if we could charge our phones conveniently

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:41.960
without having to find a plug 
and carrying a charger with us at all times?

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.340
With the proliferation of electric cars

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.860
and electronic biomedical devices like Pacemakers,

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:51.380
one vital piece of technology, 
first developed about a century ago,

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:54.660
is said to significantly improve 
the way we keep our devices powered.

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:55.980
Its called wireless charging,

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:59.760
and it's popping up in cafes, 
fast food restaurants, and IKEA furniture.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:01.760
Although there are competing standards,

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:05.560
wireless charging is power delivery 
from a power source to an electronic device

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.800
without the need for a tethered wired connection.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:11.640
Inductive charging is the most popular form 
of wireless charging for mobile devices,

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:14.580
and leading the way is the Wireless Power Consortium,

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:16.380
with their Qi Open Interface standard.

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:18.620
Using fundamentally the same technology,

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:22.940
other groups are incorporating inductive charging tech 
for use in electric vehicles.

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:25.120
Getting to the bottom of inductive charging,

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:27.860
you need to go all the way back to the mid-1800s,

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:31.960
when Michael Farraday discovered
the underlying principles of electromagnetic induction.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:35.480
He discovered that,
in the presence of an alternating magnetic field,

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:38.680
an electromotive force would be produced 
across an electric conductor.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:42.160
By the late 1800s, Nikola Tesla utilized this idea,

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:45.220
and demonstrated the phenomenon 
of resonant inductive coupling

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:47.360
by lighting an incandescent lamp wirelessly.

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:50.900
By tuning the current to match 
the resonant frequency of the coils,

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:54.240
the two coils would couple, 
providing higher efficiency power transfer.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:56.620
The discoveries that stemmed from Farraday's

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:00.640
led to the modern electrical motor 
and other unmeasurably important inventions.

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:04.880
However, for inductive charging, 
the science proved easier than implementation.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:08.000
for starters, there weren't many electronics 
that would greatly benefit

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:10.400
from such technology in the days of Nikola Tesla,

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:13.740
and due to the limitations 
in circuit board design and size constraints,

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:19.300
it wasn't until the 1990s and 2000s that the technology became viable at the consumer electronic level,

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.800
where we started seeing wireless charging toothbrushes

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:24.620
and, in 2008, wireless charging mobile phones.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:27.540
An inductive charger consists of only a few parts:

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:28.800
AC current from the wall,

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:30.780
an oscillator electrical circuit,

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.060
and the transmission coil.

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:34.960
The transmission coil is a tightly wound copper element

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:39.020
that, as the alternating current passes through, 
would produce a magnetic flux.

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:43.200
The magnetic flux desnsity is based on things like the number of turns in the wire,

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:48.000
the diameter of the transmission coil, the distance from the coil and other properties, such as current.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.300
On the receiver's end the process is basically the same, except opposite.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:55.500
In the receiver device, a coil of the same type is embedded into the charging circuit.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:59.740
The alternating magnetic field is picked up by the receiving coil, and a current is induced.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:04.000
The AC power is passed through a power rectifier 
and stabilizer to convert it into DC power

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:06.000
the phone can use to charge the battery.

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:09.500
Both the transmitter and the receiver 
have electrical resonant frequencies,

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:10.800
designed to be the same.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:14.580
For low displacement distances, 
such as a mobile phone on a charging pad,

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:18.320
it is actually LESS efficient to operate 
at the circuit resonant frequency,

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:19.700
due to heat generation.

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:24.060
However, for a larger displacement distance, such as a car parked over a charging pad,

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:25.660
operating at the resonant frequency

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:30.240
causes inductive coupling to counteract 
some of the displacement-related transmission losses.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.480
But there are many other factors that affect performance of transmission.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.880
And with no standardized design on the phones' end yet,

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:39.340
charging pads need ways 
of detecting the type of device it is charging,

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:41.060
have multiple coil arrangements

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:43.540
and control modules to alternate between modes.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:47.980
The two big questions with inductive charging 
are ones we keep coming back to:

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.400
Do people really care about the technology?

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.000
And what are the implications 
of inductive charging becoming popular?

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:59.000
Having a fully charged phone wirelessly, but nonetheless stuck to a charging pad, is barely a convenience.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:03.240
Teams are working within the Qi wireless standard 
to create devices such as the Pi Charger,

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:06.660
which can charge up to four devices 
at a range of up to a third of a meter

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:09.120
with a max power output of 10W per device.

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.160
it's completely conceivable
that the office furniture of the future

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:14.600
will have inductive charging capability built in

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:17.700
followed by most laptops,
cell phones and wireless mice.

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:19.500
But consumers need to care about it,

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:21.500
and hardware manufacturers need to see the value.

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:25.120
But there is one industry 
that inductive charging will make a meaningful impact,

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:27.980
and it happens to indirectly involve Tesla, once again.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:30.900
Going to the petrol station is a legitimate inconvenience,

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:34.100
and visiting a Tesla charging station
is an even bigger inconvenience.

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:37.760
That's why start-ups and corporations alike 
are beginning to shift their attention

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:39.080
to this lucrative opportunity.

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:41.780
Similar to charging a cell phone on a charging pad,

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.000
a transmitter would be installed in parking spaces,

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:45.900
and a receiver on the bottom of the car.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:48.860
Imagine your autonomous electric vehicle
dropping you off for dinner,

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:51.320
and seeking out a parking space
with inductive charging.

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:52.640
No attendant needed:

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:54.940
the car simply aligns itself with the transmission pad,

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:56.880
and the charging process is initiated.

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:59.200
If these industries can make this tech convenient,

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:01.720
there is a huge chance people will want to use it.

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:03.360
But is that a good thing?

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:07.280
According to a study in 2015 
conducted by the Wireless Consortium,

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:09.200
the creators of the Qi standard,

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:13.140
they found in real-world conditions, 
the Qi wireless charger had an efficiency

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:18.180
of about 59.4%, 
with their competitor coming in at just 39.6%.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:21.440
With 30 million iPhone X sold in its first quarter,

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:24.680
they have an impact of 415MWh per day,

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:26.680
if charged fully once per day.

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:28.900
With an efficiency of only 60%,

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:33.320
that would result in an increase 
of 278 MWh load on the grid,

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:36.460
which is about 25 years' 
worth of power for an average home.

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:38.840
This is only accounting for one mobile phone,

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:41.840
and not accounting for the other 2.5 billion smartphones

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:43.840
projected to be in use by 2019.

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:45.640
If all had inductive charging,

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:49.880
the impact could be over 
23,000 MWh consumed per day.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:55.100
To produce this power cleanly, the added load 
would require an additional 1,400 wind turbines.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:56.500
Just for the added load.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:05:59.540
With electric vehicles,
things can get even more interesting.

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:06.280
The Tesla Model 3 is getting between 
4.5 and 5.5 km/KWh, depending on driving habits.

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:10.360
With a daily commute of 35km, the Tesla uses 7 KWh:

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:13.240
or almost the same power usage 
as a small family home.

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:17.140
So for every EV on the road,
it's similar to adding another house on the grid.

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:19.000
And when you introduce inductive charging

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:21.900
it can equate to adding nearly two additional homes.

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:24.640
National power grids 
can likely accommodate this growth,

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:27.380
but local infrastructure may not be capable, 
or even willing,

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:31.000
to expand their capacity
for the sake of convenience to EV drivers.

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:33.000
A lot of the added stress on the grid

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:37.120
is based on the fact 
that inductive charging is an inefficient technology.

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:39.800
But that may not necessarily be true forever.

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:44.200
Oak Ridge National Lab recently demonstrated 
a 20 KWh inductive charging system

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:46.220
that operates at 90% efficiency,

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:50.220
with power delivery 
up to 3 times faster than traditional wired charging.

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:55.060
Although it's too early to know what the 
real-world wall-to-battery efficiency will look like,

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:59.000
it's still promising 
to see some of the sharpest engineers in the energy field

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:01.440
digging deeper into the technology and beyond.

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:06.720
Theoretically, it is possible to transfer energy 
over greater distances with electromagnetic waves.

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:11.400
NASA awarded one company 
$900,000 for its laser-powered climbing robot.

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:13.740
The robot had no power source on board,

00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:17.540
and managed to climb 900m 
up a cable suspended from a helicopter

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:20.100
in just under four minutes, using a high powered laser

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:23.000
to provide enough energy 
to solar cells on its undercarriage.

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:26.800
This is a fascinating technology
that could have huge potential.

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:31.600
We could have huge solar cells in space 
beaming to Earth or other satellites,

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:34.820
but right now the power loss in transmission
is far too great to be useful.

00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:38.120
and this is a problem
that is facing current technology on earth,

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:40.100
whether we want to acknowledge it or not.

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:43.360
Learning about simple core concepts like this 
that change the world

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:46.060
allows us to apply our knowledge
to real world problems.

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:48.400
if you're watching my videos, you're likely like me:

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:53.440
you're enthusiastic about science and technology 
and want to apply that knowledge to your everyday life.

00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:56.900
but to do that, 
you have to truly understand these principles yourself.

00:07:57.260 --> 00:08:01.840
Maybe you're an IT professional and want to brush up on your understanding of how computer memory works.

